Statement of Work (SoW–DNA-B)
Comparative Environmental DNA Enrichment Analysis
Issuing Organization:
 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA)
Office of Coastal Monitoring & Assessment
Performing Organization:
Academic Computing Laboratory
(ADVCS Parallel Computing Program)
Contract Reference:
NOAA-CMA-DNA-2026-B

1. Background & Mission Context
NOAA conducts long-term environmental monitoring to detect changes in microbial ecosystems that may indicate pollution events, climate impact, or ecological disruption.
Rather than absolute abundance, NOAA requires comparative analysis between two environmental samples collected under different conditions.
The agency seeks a computational solution capable of identifying taxa that are enriched or depleted between samples.

2. Scope of Work
The contractor shall analyze two FASTQ datasets, designated Sample A and Sample B, and compute differential enrichment metrics across a shared biological taxonomy.
Both datasets follow identical collection and sequencing protocols.
The application must conform to the LACC DNA Input Contract.

3. Technical Requirements
The contractor shall:
Ingest two independent FASTQ datasets.
Extract k-mers from each dataset using identical parameters.
Maintain separate k-mer counts using custom hash tables.
Compute enrichment scores per k-mer (difference or ratio).
Map k-mers to taxonomic leaf nodes.
Aggregate enrichment scores through the taxonomy tree.
Report enriched and depleted taxa.
Parallel execution using MPI + OpenMP is mandatory.

4. Required Data Structures
The solution must utilize the same ADTs as other LACC DNA analyses:
Feature Mapper: Custom hash table
Result Aggregator: Taxonomy tree
Top-K Selection: Heap-based priority queue
No additional or alternative data structures are permitted for scoring.

5. Deliverables
Executable comparative DNA analysis application
Hierarchical enrichment report
Top-K most enriched taxa
Clear documentation of enrichment metric used

6. Acceptance Criteria
The solution will be accepted if:
Enrichment calculations are consistent and reproducible
Aggregation correctly propagates values upward
Both datasets are treated symmetrically
Output format conforms to contract

7. Educational Alignment (Non-Contractual)
This work supports training in comparative analytics, distributed workloads, and algorithmic fairness in scientific computing.


